9. Testing & Debugging (Production-Grade – Salesforce Developer)

This section is written the way real orgs expect it: scalable, CI-friendly, bulk-safe, and audit-proof.

9.1 Test Classes
Structure (Enterprise Standard)

A production-grade test class is not just coverage — it validates behavior, edge cases, and bulk scenarios.

@isTest
private class AccountServiceTest {

    @testSetup
    static void setupData() {
        List<Account> accs = new List<Account>();
        for(Integer i=0; i<10; i++){
            accs.add(new Account(Name = 'Test Acc ' + i));
        }
        insert accs;
    }

    @isTest
    static void testMethod_BasicFlow() {
        List<Account> accs = [SELECT Id, Name FROM Account];

        Test.startTest();
        AccountService.processAccounts(accs);
        Test.stopTest();

        List<Account> updated = [SELECT Id, Processed__c FROM Account];
        System.assertEquals(true, updated[0].Processed__c);
    }
}
Key Principles (Production Reality)
1. Use @testSetup ALWAYS (for reusable data)
Runs once → reused across methods
Reduces execution time
Makes tests deterministic
2. Never rely on org data
@isTest(SeeAllData=false)
Default in modern Salesforce
Ensures portability across orgs (CI/CD safe)
Test Data Creation (Best Practice Patterns)
Pattern 1: Factory Class (MANDATORY in large orgs)
public class TestDataFactory {
    public static Account createAccount(Boolean doInsert) {
        Account acc = new Account(Name = 'Factory Acc');
        if(doInsert) insert acc;
        return acc;
    }
}

Usage:

Account acc = TestDataFactory.createAccount(true);
Pattern 2: Bulk Data Creation

Always test bulk behavior:

List<Account> accs = new List<Account>();
for(Integer i=0; i<200; i++){
    accs.add(new Account(Name='Bulk ' + i));
}
insert accs;
3. Test All Branches (Critical)

If your code has:

if(condition) { ... } else { ... }

You must test:

condition = true
condition = false
9.2 Code Coverage
75% Requirement (Reality vs Myth)
Salesforce requires 75% org-wide
But real companies expect: 85–95% meaningful coverage
Meaningful Coverage (THIS is what matters)

Bad:

System.assert(true);

Good:

System.assertEquals(expectedValue, actualValue);
Coverage Strategy
Layer	What to Test
Service Layer	Business logic
Trigger	Before/After logic
Integration	Callouts (mocked)
Edge Cases	Nulls, limits
Anti-Patterns (Avoid)
Writing tests just for coverage
Not asserting results
Testing only happy path
Ignoring bulk scenarios
9.3 Advanced Testing
Test.startTest() / Test.stopTest()
Purpose:
Resets governor limits
Executes async code
Test.startTest();

MyFutureClass.doAsyncWork();

Test.stopTest(); // Forces execution
Async Testing (Critical)
Future Methods
Queueable
Batch Apex
Test.startTest();
System.enqueueJob(new MyQueueable());
Test.stopTest();
Mock Callouts (MANDATORY for integrations)

Salesforce does NOT allow real HTTP callouts in tests

Implement Mock
@isTest
global class MockHttpResponse implements HttpCalloutMock {
    
    global HTTPResponse respond(HTTPRequest req) {
        HttpResponse res = new HttpResponse();
        res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        res.setBody('{"status":"success"}');
        res.setStatusCode(200);
        return res;
    }
}
Use Mock
Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, new MockHttpResponse());

Test.startTest();
MyIntegrationClass.callAPI();
Test.stopTest();
Named Credentials Testing
Use mock
Do NOT hardcode endpoints
Always simulate response
